{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 36, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli ruled the County of Tripoli from 1154 until his death in 1199. He was a significant figure in the Crusades and a powerful ruler in the Levant navigating complex political landscapes involving the Holy Roman Empire the Kingdom of Sicily and various Muslim dynasties. Born around 1120 Raymond inherited the County at a young age succeeding his father Raymond II. His early reign was marked by consolidating his power and dealing with internal challenges including rebellions from local lords.\n\n*Early Reign and Consolidation of Power*\n\nRaymond III faced immediate challenges upon assuming the County. He had to quell internal dissent and assert his authority over the local population. He skillfully employed a combination of diplomacy and military force to achieve this. He also focused on strengthening the County's defenses building fortifications and maintaining a standing army. His efforts were crucial in ensuring the stability of Tripoli during a turbulent period. He dealt with various rebellious factions and maintained a delicate balance of power amongst the different groups within his territory.\n\nRaymond III's reign coincided with the rise of Saladin and the Ayyubid dynasty. He engaged in numerous conflicts with Saladin's forces defending Tripoli and its surrounding territories. These battles were strategically important in maintaining Christian control of the Levant. He often allied himself with other Crusader states like the Kingdom of Jerusalem to counter the Ayyubid threat. His military campaigns were often costly but they successfully preserved the County's independence for a considerable period.\n\n*Diplomacy and Alliances*\n\nRaymond III was a skilled diplomat adept at forging alliances and navigating complex political relationships. He maintained close ties with the Holy Roman Emperor Frederick I Barbarossa and the Kings of Sicily particularly Roger II and his successors. These alliances provided crucial military and financial support to Tripoli. He also engaged in diplomatic negotiations with various Muslim rulers seeking to maintain peaceful relations where possible. He understood the importance of maintaining a balance of power in the region and used diplomacy to achieve his goals. He strategically positioned Tripoli to benefit from the rivalries between different Muslim factions.\n\n*Legacy and Death*\n\nRaymond III died in 1199 leaving behind a County of Tripoli that was relatively stable and prosperous. He was remembered as a capable ruler a skilled military leader and a shrewd diplomat. His reign played a vital role in preserving Christian presence in the Levant during a time of intense conflict. The County of Tripoli continued to thrive under his successors maintaining its importance as a key Crusader state. His legacy is one of resilience and strategic leadership in the face of overwhelming odds. He is considered one of the most important rulers of the County of Tripoli.\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.0011152960360050201}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -2.861018856492592e-06}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁ruled", "logprob": -0.2664777338504791}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.12502680718898773}, {"token": "▁County", "logprob": -0.060070887207984924}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁from", "logprob": -0.00020692592079285532}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -2.8729025871143676e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -7.748573807475623e-06}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -2.8967437174287625e-05}, {"token": "4", "logprob": -0.001587560516782105}, {"token": "▁until", "logprob": -0.02715086005628109}, {"token": "▁his", "logprob": -0.000284154579276219}, {"token": "▁death", "logpr